  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Monday)\nIssued at 1114 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nAn upper level low continues to spin over Texas with a leading\nshortwave digging over southern Arkansas. Due to a lack of low\nlevel moisture, and overall weakness of the wave, no\nprecipitation is reaching the surface near the Mid-South this\nevening. Light reflectivity returns are present over south-\ncentral Louisiana and Central Texas. Cirrus continues to stream\nacross north Mississippi, southeast Arkansas and southwest\nTennessee. This deck of cirrus should disrupt fog potential in\nthe aforementioned areas, but patchy to locally dense fog could\ndevelop across the rest of the area. Areas west of the\nMississippi River are more likely to fog this evening, but expect\nriver basins elsewhere to produce steam fog.\n\nThe aforementioned upper low over Texas will cross the Mid-South\nthrough Wednesday, allowing temperatures to climb into the low to\nmid 70s and perhaps return 60 degree dewpoints. A deeper trough\nwill emerge from the Rockies concurrently, and take a dive\nsoutheasterly towards the region. It will stretch a cold front to\nsweep across the area on Wednesday afternoon and evening. A Wind\nAdvisory is not anticipated at this time despite a tightening\npressure gradient. As we have lift and shear heading this way,\nSPC has highlighted northern portions of the Mid-South in a\nMarginal Risk for severe thunderstorms. Based on probabilities\nfrom the REFS, this is predominantly shear driven. Probabilities\nof severe thunderstorm ingredients (>500 J/kg of CAPE and 30 kts\nof bulk shear) are low to medium (20-60%). These probabilities\ndrastically decline to 10-30% for >750J/kg of CAPE. Deep, moist\nconvection seems unlikely with PWs projected to be an inch or\nless (in the 75th percentile for this time of year), but the\nshear profile remains impressive enough to warrant the extra\nattention.\n\nWhile the cold front will sweep through rather quickly,\nsubsidence will lag behind. High pressure will encompass the\nregion by Friday evening and plummet temperatures near or below\nfreezing, with eastern areas of the region likely (20-50% chance)\nto fall below 28F. The cold should be short lived as an upper\nridge begins to build from the west.\n\nDNM",
